PAGES['/projects/hidalga/'] = dict(title='Healthcare AI Product Strategy | Joshua Upshaw, PhD', description='A case study in translating oncology prior authorization challenges into healthcare AI research and product requirements.', content='''
<section class="page-hero dark-section"><div class="container"><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← All projects</a><p class="eyebrow light">HEALTHCARE AI · PRODUCT & R&D LEADERSHIP</p><h1>From clinical friction to <span class="gradient-text">product direction.</span></h1><p>Hidalga Technologies: investigating oncology prior authorization and designing evidence-aware technology around real clinical workflows.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container article-grid"><article class="article-body"><h2>The challenge</h2><p>Oncology teams must navigate fragmented documentation, payer requirements, authorization review, and manual follow-up. The problem extends beyond form filling: it depends on clinical evidence, responsibilities, workflow state, and safe human decisions.</p><h2>My responsibilities</h2><p>As founder, CEO, and NSF principal investigator, I defined research and product priorities, owned the roadmap, translated stakeholder findings into requirements, guided contributors, and conducted analytical research.</p><h2>Approach</h2><ul><li>Conducted structured healthcare stakeholder discovery and translated recurring workflow problems into product priorities.</li><li>Defined concepts for clinical evidence readiness, payer-policy evaluation, denial-risk prediction, review controls, and traceable documentation.</li><li>Developed requirements for human oversight, provenance, uncertainty, role-based access, and auditability.</li><li>Established research objectives, validation criteria, and prospective pilot measurement plans.</li></ul><h2>Evidence and outcomes</h2><p>Directed an NSF SBIR Phase I award of $304,935. The retrospective research program covered 24,167 oncology patients and 200,513 authorization case records. Findings informed the product roadmap and a program of further evaluation.</p><p><strong>Evidence boundary:</strong> Research performance, workflow simulations, and development prototypes are not equivalent to validated clinical deployment or realized efficiency gains.</p><h2>Why it matters</h2><p>The project illustrates how careful clinical discovery and scientific evaluation can produce testable product requirements and guide technology investment without overstating implementation readiness.</p></article><aside class="fact-panel"><p class="eyebrow">AT A GLANCE</p><dl><dt>Role</dt><dd>Founder, CEO & Principal Investigator</dd><dt>Field</dt><dd>Healthcare AI, oncology operations</dd><dt>Program</dt><dd>NSF SBIR Phase I</dd><dt>Research setting</dt><dd>Retrospective feasibility study</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href="/projects/clinical-ai/">Explore the clinical AI analysis ↗</a></aside></div></section>
''')

PAGES['/projects/clinical-ai/'] = dict(title='Clinical Data Science & AI Research | Joshua Upshaw, PhD', description='Retrospective oncology prior authorization research using Python, NLP, and machine-learning validation.', content='''
<section class="page-hero dark-section"><div class="container"><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← All projects</a><p class="eyebrow light">CLINICAL DATA SCIENCE · MACHINE LEARNING</p><h1>Clinical data into <span class="gradient-text">defensible evidence.</span></h1><p>Research pipelines, clinical information extraction, rare-outcome prediction, and transparent validation in retrospective oncology data.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container article-grid"><article class="article-body"><h2>Research question</h2><p>Can heterogeneous oncology EHR and authorization records be transformed into analysis-ready evidence suitable for studying documentation readiness, authorization outcomes, and administrative workflows?</p><h2>My contribution</h2><p>I personally developed and evaluated research pipelines and predictive models in Python, using data-quality checks, harmonization, structured and text extraction, cohort definition, and model evaluation.</p><h2>Methods</h2><ul><li>Prepared retrospective records across 24,167 patients, 200,513 authorization case rows, and 148,703 clinical notes.</li><li>Combined structured clinical data and text-based information extraction, with field-level evaluation.</li><li>Evaluated rare-outcome denial-risk prediction using patient-grouped validation, PR-AUC and ROC-AUC, calibration, and temporal checks.</li><li>Examined retrospective workflow timing and simulated potential improvements under defined assumptions.</li></ul><h2>Selected results</h2><div class="mini-metrics"><div><strong>0.97</strong><span>Mean F1 across six extracted fields</span></div><div><strong>0.88</strong><span>Patient-grouped denial ROC-AUC</span></div><div><strong>0.29</strong><span>Denial PR-AUC, retrospective test</span></div></div><p><strong>Evidence boundary:</strong> Metrics reflect retrospective evaluation. Workflow improvements were modeled, not prospectively observed in routine clinical operations. Research data and protected information are not posted here.</p><h2>Decision relevance</h2><p>Model evaluation informed what appeared technically feasible, where data quality constrained interpretation, and what prospective instrumentation would be necessary before live clinical claims.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/jupshaw/joshua-upshaw-research-development-portfolio/tree/main/projects/05_clinical_ai_oncology" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the public research documentation ↗</a></p></article><aside class="fact-panel"><p class="eyebrow">AT A GLANCE</p><dl><dt>Methods</dt><dd>Python, pandas, clinical NLP, gradient boosting</dd><dt>Validation</dt><dd>Patient-grouped, temporal, class-imbalance-aware</dd><dt>Evidence type</dt><dd>Retrospective feasibility</dd><dt>Deployment status</dt><dd>No completed production clinical validation</dd></dl></aside></div></section>
''')

PAGES['/projects/neuroscience/'] = dict(title='Cognitive Neuroscience Research | Joshua Upshaw, PhD', description='EEG, ERP, and behavioral experiments investigating smartphone notifications and cognitive control.', content='''
<section class="page-hero dark-section"><div class="container"><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← All projects</a><p class="eyebrow light">COGNITIVE NEUROSCIENCE · EXPERIMENTAL RESEARCH</p><h1>How technology competes <span class="gradient-text">for attention.</span></h1><p>Human neuroscience research using EEG, behavioral experiments, and eye tracking to understand distraction and cognitive control.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container article-grid"><article class="article-body"><h2>The question</h2><p>What happens to attention and cognitive control when everyday smartphone notifications compete with ongoing cognitive demands? Can brief behavioral interventions modify those effects?</p><h2>Research design and execution</h2><p>I designed and conducted human experimental studies using EEG/ERP, spectral analysis, stimulus timing, behavioral performance, and quantitative inference. This work included a randomized study with 87 participants and a preregistered 50-participant eye-tracking investigation.</p><h2>Selected work</h2><ul><li>Studied electrophysiological and behavioral responses to smartphone notifications during cognitive tasks.</li><li>Evaluated cognitive control following a brief mindfulness induction using frequency-domain EEG measures.</li><li>Developed an emotional-attention eye-tracking experiment examining fixation, saccade, pupil, blink, and behavioral measures.</li><li>Communicated results and limitations through peer-reviewed publications and research presentations.</li></ul><h2>Published evidence</h2><p><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biopsycho.2023.108725" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Biological Psychology (2023) ↗</a></p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0277220" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PLOS ONE (2022) ↗</a></p><h2>Practical relevance</h2><p>This research illustrates disciplined experimental design, neural-signal quality management, and translation of cognitive science into human-centered technology questions.</p></article><aside class="fact-panel"><p class="eyebrow">AT A GLANCE</p><dl><dt>Research domain</dt><dd>Attention and cognitive control</dd><dt>Methods</dt><dd>EEG, ERP, eye tracking, behavior</dd><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Peer-reviewed, first-author studies</dd><dt>Affiliation</dt><dd>University of Arkansas</dd></dl></aside></div></section>
''')

